{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-compat","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The project has a hard Bash 3.2 portability requirement (BASH_AUTHORING.md), and the new probe code introduces several constructs that deserve explicit scrutiny against that forbidden list.","prompt_body":"Audit `scripts/check-reviewers.sh` and `scripts/test-check-reviewers.sh` strictly against the Bash 3.2 compatibility rules in `BASH_AUTHORING.md`. Check for forbidden constructs: `declare -A`/`declare -n`/`local -n`, `mapfile`/`readarray`, `${var^^}`/`${var,,}`, `&>>`, `coproc`. Verify that `printf -v` (Bash 3.1+), `(( ))` arithmetic, `${arr[@]+\"${arr[@]}\"}` empty-array guards, and `SECONDS` builtin reset are all valid in Bash 3.2. Confirm that the `case`-based env-var normalization pattern replaces any 4+-only constructs. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"probe-lifecycle","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The new probe functions introduce background PID management, timeout kill sequences, and temp-file cleanup that are notoriously easy to get wrong in shell — resource leaks on timeout paths are invisible to static review.","prompt_body":"Examine the background-process lifecycle in `larch_run_one_cursor_probe` and `larch_run_one_codex_probe` in `scripts/check-reviewers.sh`. Verify that `probe_out` and `probe_side` temp files are removed in every exit path: success, non-auth failure, auth failure after retries, and the timeout-kill path. Check whether `wait \"$probe_pid\"` is safe after `kill \"$probe_pid\"` when the process has already exited (race between kill and natural exit). Verify that resetting `SECONDS=0` inside a function doesn't interfere with the parent shell's elapsed-time tracking or with the outer retry loop. Check whether zombie or orphaned background processes can accumulate if the parent script is killed mid-probe with no `trap`. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"stamp-reliability","focus_area":"edge-cases","weight":1,"rationale":"The TTL stamp tests use `touch -t`/`touch -A` backdating with `|| true` fallback, meaning a platform where neither flag works silently becomes a false-passing test; the stamp path also embeds USER without sanitization.","prompt_body":"Review the TTL stamp read/write logic in `scripts/check-reviewers.sh` and the stamp-expiry test cases in `scripts/test-check-reviewers.sh`. Check whether the `mktemp`+`mv` atomic write fails silently if the tmp directory is on a different filesystem from the stamp destination. Examine the `touch -t 200001010000 … || touch -A … || true` pattern: if both commands fail (e.g. a container without `touch -t` support), the stamp is not backdated but the test continues, producing a false pass — assess whether this is guarded. Check that the stamp path `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/larch-cursor-present-${USER:-larch}.stamp` handles users whose names contain path-separator or special characters. Verify the mtime detection fallback (`stat -c %Y` vs `stat -f %m`) covers the case where neither stat variant succeeds before the freshness comparison. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
